# Staging replica — a full copy of production that acts on nothing.
#
# Copy to ~/tapedeck/.env on the staging host. `deploy/staging-restore.sh`
# refuses to install a production snapshot on a host whose .env does not set
# TAPEDECK_REPLICA=1, so this file is load-bearing rather than documentation.

# ── The switch ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Forwards no listens, pushes no now-playing, publishes no actor, and asks
# MusicBrainz nothing. See src/replica.rs for what each gate is and why it sits
# where it does.
TAPEDECK_REPLICA=1

# ── Deliberately unset: MUSICBRAINZ_CONTACT ─────────────────────────────────
# Replica mode already refuses the network, and leaving this unset is a second
# lock on the same door: an unconfigured contact makes the client refuse every
# request at startup, loudly, while the cache keeps answering. Staging and
# production share one public address, and MetaBrainz blocks by IP at the
# connection level with no status code to say so — which cost two days to
# diagnose on 2026-08-27. Do not set it here.

# ── Identity ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Must differ from production. Getting it wrong publishes an http:// OAuth
# issuer, which a conformant client refuses outright and which presents as a
# connector that mysteriously has no tools (v0.63.2).
PUBLIC_URL=https://staging.tapedeck.example

# The encryption key. Production's snapshot carries ciphertext in
# `user_sources.credentials`, and the restore script deletes the rows that
# matter — but a *different* key here means staging can never decrypt anything
# it inherited even if a table is missed. Generate one: openssl rand -base64 32
TAPEDECK_SECRET_KEY=

# ── Ordinary ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
DATABASE_URL=scrobbles.db
PORT=8080
RUST_LOG=info
